content.text:
Two workers are running, both on Fable at medium effort.

- **The planner of the next batch, climb batch 30.** It plans the numerals leftovers and the parallelism repairs together. The parallelism part covers:
  - a group of forked tasks joins every task before it ends on an exception;
  - a call's arguments fork, so that reductions run in parallel;
  - the defects of the compiled `atomic`, the livelock among them.

  It leaves out the two decisions that are yours: unboxed arrays inside `atomic`, and strong atomicity. If a repair can't do without one of them, I bring it to you as a question before the launch. The planner may split the work into two runs if one merge can't hold it cleanly.
- **The review on `u = 0`.** Your confirmed design already gives the result: true, because the numeral converts at compile time to the other operand's type. So the review asks only how both paths get there. I bring it to you only if a way bends one of your decisions.

**On the Fable review before the launch:** I'd keep one fresh reader, but narrower. Its worth was a second reader who isn't anchored on the plan, more than a higher tier. Last night's batch shows the case: a plan can follow its sources faithfully and still carry a wrong premise. So the reader would do a check, not a review:
- each citation at its source;
- each task against your decisions and your words;
- the open choices sorted into yours and not yours;
- the merge risk.

It would not rewrite the record. I expect about 0.3M tokens, against about 0.5M for the old review. If you'd rather drop it, say so and I launch straight from the plan.

**One change to what I said earlier:** the skill compression waits until this batch has landed. The landing's own skill update rewrites the same three parts, and the batch's workers should read one stable text.

I'll report when the plan is in, with its size and cost.

