A refinement of point 2 of my last message, in the curator's words: "by multi-threaded, I mean make it shaped using the fork join that we will be using." So shape the multi-threaded variant as the Fortress library and its compiled runtime shape parallel work, so that its time is the best that compiled Fortress could reach with its own structure:

- a parallel loop over a range splits the range in halves, recursively, as the one library's parallel range does, and stops at a grain size, below which it runs in sequence (`Library/RangeInternals.fss`: read how the `ZZ32` range splits and its `grainSize`, 16 today); the halves are forked tasks on a `java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool`, as the compiled runtime forks them (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/runtimeSystem/`, `BaseTask` and its `forkIfProfitable`);
- a reduction, such as a sum or a dot product, joins the results of its two halves, as the library's reductions do, and does not write a shared accumulator;
- the matrix product follows the library's `Matrix` product (`mul` in `Library/FortressLibrary.fss`: it halves the largest dimension recursively and forks the two halves); say what grain you gave it and what the library's own would be.

Time it at the library's grain as it is today and at one or two other grains, at 1, 2 and 4 threads, and say per kernel where the fork/join shape loses to the plain loop at the model's sizes. Keep the result within the checks' tolerances, and say whether the halving changes the order of the floating-point sums against the plain loop. The one-thread plain-loop program stays the baseline.

